A||||14||btoth||Just got new engine installed... have some concerns||||4||
Z||000000||btoth||05-14-2001||12:47 PM||||Well, I just picked up my 86 GT (AUTOMATIC)<BR>from the shop.  It got a remanufactured 2.8L engine installed.  (Supposedly has a performance cam and some other goodies over an OEM one.)<P>Well, the engine seem's great.  Really quiet and seems like more power over the old worn out one.  But I have some concerns:<P>When I had called last week to check up on it, they said everything was done except for the timing.... well, when I brought it home today and checked the timing (with the computer in diagnostic mode) it wasn't even near the 10 degree mark.  The only reason I checked it was because the idle was fluctuating all over the place and my shifting felt weird.  So, I adjusted it as good as I could to the 10 degree mark.<P>After adjusting the timing, the idle smoothed out... better, but not perfect... sometimes it takes a dive towards the 500RPM range when in drive and idling.  But my main concern is this:  when I'm accelerating, I noticed a slight lag at around 2500RPM.. which I figured out must have been a shift from 1st to 2nd... seemed way too smooth though.  But it seems like it's just shifting too early no matter how fast I accelerate (not pushing too hard seeing as how it's a brand new engine).<P>BTW, when adjusting the timing, is it necessary to have the car in DRIVE or is PARK just fine??<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000001||Formula88||05-14-2001||12:52 PM||||Car in Park, but don't forget to ground the A and B terminal in your ALDL connector.  Then set the timing.  The procedure is covered in most any service manual.||||reg||1
Z||000002||btoth||05-14-2001||12:54 PM||||Yep, did that, ground the terminals, got the blink engine light... have become quite good at it over the last year. (Along with tearing down the top of my engine.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> )  I think one of my manuals said to set it in DRIVE but I just did it in PARK anyway cuz I'm the only one home at the moment.<p>[This message has been edited by btoth (edited 05-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Steve Normington||05-14-2001||01:59 PM||||The Drive vs Park may make a difference since the engine has a greater load when it is in Drive with the brakes on. Also, the new cam may require different timing to smooth out the idle if it is a more radical grind.<P>------------------<BR>I don't car if my next Fiero is a 2M Hamster, I'm not swapping the engine.||||reg||1
Z||000004||84Bill||05-14-2001||02:21 PM||||Shifting funny? Sounds like the throttle valve cable needs to be adjusted.||||reg||3
Z||000005||Raydar||05-14-2001||03:09 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 84Bill:<BR><B>Shifting funny? Sounds like the throttle valve cable needs to be adjusted.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Exactly!<BR>If the T-V cable is improperly adjusted, or not connected at all, the car will shift very early, and very soft. Allows for much slippage. Not healthy for the trans, at all.<BR>The 500 rpm "dip" at idle will probably sort itself out, once it's driven for a little while.<P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||1
Z||000006||btoth||05-14-2001||04:41 PM||||Well I'll try setting the timing in drive later tonight and see what difference that makes.  <P>The guy did say that he had to completely remake the throttle cable and downshift cable because they were bad.  It downshifts great and maybe the way it upshifts is ok too and I just don't know how a "new" Fiero drives like.  Maybe I'll just let the engine break in and let the computer set again and see what happens before I think about it too hard.<P>But the new engine sure is nice!!<p>[This message has been edited by btoth (edited 05-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||btoth||05-15-2001||11:31 AM||||<BR>Another question:  would it make sense that after having the engine and everything removed from the car that after putting the new in that I would need a rear alignment?? My back end feels like it's shifting back and forth (left to right) whenever I get up around 40mph.<P>Second question for the day... the guy that did the intstall said that he had to make (or remake?) the throttle and downshift cables.  he didn't charge me for them because he had already quoted me on the price but my shifting still seems to shift too early.  Even when accelerating at a decent rate it still wants to shift out of first at around 2500rpm and then get out of 2nd around the same point, instead of letting the rpm's climb over the 3000rpm like it used to do.  (is this normal?  i'm not flooring it because it's a new engine, but it doesn't seem right).  I looked at the Fiero Store and they have the throttle and kick-down cables for the automatic V6 and I might try getting them if you think that could be my problem... how much of a pain in the @$$ is it too install the throttle cable?  Or do you think that it would be best to let the engine break in a bit before messing with it?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||btoth||05-15-2001||12:52 PM||||I was reading through the factory service manual and noticed that it says that if the T.V. cable is misadjusted that that can lead to high oil pressure.  And, now that I think of it, my oil pressure as been up near the 80 mark and doesn't seem to want to come down below where the 70 might be (esitimated).  Does this make sense what I just said?<P>I'm going to give the shop a call later that did the install and *try* to explain some of this stuff to them... but I'm sick of them assuming that I know nothing about this car.||||reg||1
Z||000009||87GTZ34||05-15-2001||01:01 PM||||Btoth - that is the transmission oil pressure is high when TV is mis-adjusted.  Your oil pressure is high because your bearing tolerances are like new!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> That is a good thing.  On the timing and sputter I've read recently about a few folks with wierd symptoms and when they couldn't get it timed correctly, they found out that the outer ring on the harmonic balancer has shifted and the timing marks are no longer accurate.  To check this, take out #1 plug and put a new straight drinking straw in it, rotate the crankshaft to TDC (yours will turn hard w/ a new motor).  Make sure the staw is moving freely so it doesn't get caught between the piston and cyl.  Watch the straw while turning the crank (2 people needed) to see if the straw tops out when the mark reaches TDC.  This is not accurate to the degree of course but should be a good enough check to see if the HB is way off.||||reg||1
Z||000010||btoth||05-15-2001||01:39 PM||||The trans oil thing makes sense  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> duh.  And yeah, I guess it does make sense that my oil pressure is high now... seeing as how my bearings were pretty much shot in the old engine.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>It's probably just mis-adjusted cables causing the low rpm shifting, which shouldn't be too big of a thing for them to get right.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> (I hope.)<P>Now I just need decent breaks to stop the car once it gets going.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||88formula||05-15-2001||05:28 PM||||If the guy tried to make a new bracket for the TV cable that holds the TV cable to the engine than chances are that it won't work right or shift like it did before. Also it is adjustable and you can adjust it to make it shift late or early at any given rpm. It will also shift harder or softer depending on where you adjust it.   <P>They are very touchy!<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||fyerstarter||05-15-2001||07:50 PM||||About your alignment question I think there are many different ways of removing the cradle and engine, sometimes with the whole back struts still on, etc. etc. So it's possible that the back end could be out of alignment now depending on how he did it.||||reg||1
Z||000013||87GTZ34||05-15-2001||09:30 PM||||I'm assuming when they made up the TV cable, they just replaced the actual cable and reused the housing, brackets etc.  If that is the case, then the only critical thing is that they made it close to the original length.  A little longer (and I mean little) won't hurt as it can be adjusted out.  Too short would be a problem, but from the sounds of your symptoms (early shift) that is not the case.  Here is something you can try.  On the throttle body there is a ratchet lock built into the TV cable casing.  Press the button looking thing 'IN' to adjust it.  That is where the TV  adjustment is made.  Count how many ratchet locks (lines) are showing b4 you start adjusting and write it down.  Next lengthen the cable 2 lines at a time and drive it every time you adjust it.  Don't get anxious and move it more in 1 test because chances are you'll have to start over again.  Unfortunately, you need to do this at operating temp so it will be hot (use a rag so you don't get burnt).  If you have a helper, then warm the car up, have the helper hold the throttle wide open (using a rag & with the car turned off of course) and take all the slack out of the TV cable with the adjuster.  If you can't get it right put it back where it was b4 you started, too loose is much better than too tight (high trans pressure issue).   GL||||reg||1
Z||000014||btoth||05-16-2001||12:10 PM||||Well, took my car out around the block last night to show my dad how it was shifting... well, it wouldn't get out of 1st!  So I checked the cables out and found that the "kick-down" cable (is this also the TV cable that you were refering to?) looked like it was pulled out from the part that attaches to the transmission.  Kinda had a frayed look with little wires poking out around the center wire that goes down inside the trans.  (A digital camera would really be nice right now.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)  Anyway, I wiggled the cable and it went back to shifting really early.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  So I'm <I>guessing</I> that it's just broken somehow.<P>Talked to the guy that did the install and he told me to just order the part (got it from the Fiero Store) and he'd fix it.  He did say that he aligned the read-end, but he'd check it again just to make sure it's ok.<P>Sigh.  Maybe I'll actually be able to drive my car by the end of the week.<P>BTW, I printed out the how-to on adjusting the cable just incase... thanks for the info!||||reg||1
